  "body": {
    "id": "thm:pooled-wald-certified",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Assume \\(p\\geq3\\), fix \\(\\iota\\), and let \\(\\mathfrak C(\\Xi)=\\mathtt{CERTIFIED}\\). Suppose the true pooled model has \\(\\boldsymbol D_0\\in\\mathfrak G\\) and \\(\\theta_0\\in\\Theta^{\\mathrm{in}}_{\\boldsymbol D_0}\\), the normalized allocation obeys \\(\\rho\\in\\Delta_J^{\\mathbb Q}\\) with \\(\\rho_j>0\\) for every row and \\(n_{Sj}/N_S\\to\\rho_j\\) for \\(S\\in\\{A,B\\}\\), and the weighting and nondegeneracy conditions hold. As \\(\\min(N_A,N_B)\\to\\infty\\), the declared lexicographic selector satisfies \\(\\Pr(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol D}=\\boldsymbol D_0)\\to1\\), while \\(\\sqrt{N_B}(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol\\tau}-\\boldsymbol\\tau)\\Rightarrow\\mathcal N(0,V_\\tau(\\boldsymbol D_0,\\theta_0;\\iota))\\). Plug-in covariance is consistent and scalar Wald intervals have pointwise coverage \\(1-\\alpha\\). The conclusion separately uses certified wrong-graph separation, certified global within-graph uniqueness, certified local nonsingularity, certified positive inference-box clearance, the normalized row allocation, and the deterministic selector. Moreover \\(\\lVert\\Sigma_g\\rVert_2\\leq\\lVert\\operatorname{diag}(g)\\rVert_2^2\\lVert M^+\\rVert_2^2\\lVert\\operatorname{diag}(h)^{-1}\\rVert_2^2\\lVert\\Sigma_h\\rVert_2\\).",
